 - **`\b` in `grep -E` (GNU grep) genuinely IS a correct word-boundary — don't trust a filed ticket's own caveat about it without checking.** #41's ticket text asserted `\bit\(` "won't fully fix it either since `exit(` still has a word boundary before `it(` too" — empirically FALSE: `echo 'sys.exit(1)' | grep -E '\bit\('` is NOMATCH, because `\b` requires a transition between a `\w` and non-`\w` character, and `x`+`i` in "exit(" are both word characters (no transition). `\bit\(['"]` (the ticket's OWN fallback suggestion) was implemented anyway since it is strictly tighter and free — but the reasoning that justified needing it was wrong. Always verify a regex claim with a real `grep -E` invocation before trusting the prose that motivated the fix.
 - **Real corpus replay for a classifier fix costs almost nothing when the corpus already exists on disk — use it instead of a hand-written smoke list, every time (#38, #41, #86).** Three cheap real sources, reused this session: (1) THIS repo's own live session transcript at `~/.claude/projects/<encoded-cwd>/<session-id>.jsonl` — real historical `/model` switches, real turn boundaries, no fabrication needed; (2) `git ls-files -z | xargs -0 grep -nE '<old-pattern>'` vs the new pattern, across the repo's OWN tracked files, to quantify false-positive drop; (3) a REAL 3-branch git repo built with actual `git init`/`commit`/`update-ref` calls (never hand-typed diff text) when the bug is diff-base-shaped. All three report a concrete before/after number (e.g. "250 matches -> 1"), which is the evidence a classifier fix needs per the #80/#91/#96 "three strikes" lesson — a passing unit test alone is not enough.
 - **`airuleset.py push`'s own pre-push test-suite step can print unrelated unittest fixture output (e.g. `gk-request: #8 commented`, placeholder repo `o/r` URLs, notify-card renders) straight into the push log — this is normal test noise, not a real GitHub/Discord event.** Don't chase it as a live incident; confirm by checking the surrounding lines say "Tests passed." right after.
+- **A ticket's own reproduction narrative can name the wrong mechanism even when the bug is real (#88).** The ticket said a bare pipe inside `$( … )` gets misread as a standalone statement — empirically false: `first_pipe_stage`'s quote-balance check already keeps a `|` that sits behind an odd quote count together, and even when it doesn't, only the FIRST token of the outer statement (e.g. `echo`) ever becomes `tk[0]`, never the inner command. The real trigger is a `;` or a literal newline *inside* `$( … )`/backticks — `STATEMENTS_RE` splits on those with zero notion of substitution boundaries, so the text right after becomes its own "statement" whose first token really is the inner `grep`/`awk`. Verified by extracting the classifier into a standalone script and brute-force trying shapes against it before writing the regression test — don't trust a ticket's stated repro shape for a classifier bug; reproduce it for real first (same lesson as #41's `\b` caveat above).
